      # ----- Home Assistant auth / ban detection (2026-10-21) -----
      # The failure that motivated these two: the 10-18 refresh-token rotation
      # deleted the entry behind three tokens still in service, so their
      # requests started failing auth, HA banned the source address, and from
      # then on EVERY request from that address got 403 no matter how valid the
      # token was. Because the private subnet egresses through tailscale-gw
      # (192.168.86.16), one banned address silently took the whole SDN out of
      # Home Assistant, and the metrics scrape with it. Nothing reported it.
      #
      # These read HA's own log stream (job="home-assistant", shipped by
      # roles/ha_log_shipper), not systemd-journal.

      # (a) HA banned an address. `Banned IP <ip> ...` comes from
      # homeassistant.components.http.ban and is the only record of the state
      # change; afterwards that client just gets 403 forever.
      - uid: ha_ip_banned
        title: Home Assistant banned a client IP
        condition: C
        data:
          - refId: A
            relativeTimeRange:
              from: 900
              to: 0
            datasourceUid: loki
            model:
              datasource:
                type: loki
                uid: loki
              # Range query + reduce + math, not an instant query with a
              # classic threshold -- see the duplicate-label bug documented on
              # log_oom_kill.
              expr: 'sum by (host) (count_over_time({job="home-assistant"} |~ "Banned IP " [10m]))'
              instant: false
              range: true
              intervalMs: 1000
              maxDataPoints: 43200
              refId: A
          - refId: B
            datasourceUid: __expr__
            model:
              datasource:
                name: Expression
                type: __expr__
                uid: __expr__
              expression: A
              reducer: last
              type: reduce
              intervalMs: 1000
              maxDataPoints: 43200
              refId: B
          - refId: C
            datasourceUid: __expr__
            model:
              datasource:
                name: Expression
                type: __expr__
                uid: __expr__
              expression: "$B > 0"
              type: math
              intervalMs: 1000
              maxDataPoints: 43200
              refId: C
        noDataState: OK
        execErrState: Alerting
        for: 1m
        annotations:
          summary: "Home Assistant banned a client IP"
          description: "Home Assistant logged `Banned IP ...` on {{ '{{' }} $labels.host {{ '}}' }} in the last 10 minutes. A ban is a state change, not an error line: from now on EVERY request from that address returns 403 regardless of token validity, which is how the 2026-10-18/20 bans silently broke the SDN's API consumers (the subnet egresses via tailscale-gw 192.168.86.16, so one ban takes every container out of HA). The ban line names the address -- decide whether it was hostile traffic or a stale/rotated credential, then clear it: prune that entry from Home Assistant's ip_bans.yaml (keep a .bak, as was done on 2026-10-21) and restart HA. If the banned address is the exporter host, expect the metrics scrape to be 403ing too (see ha_metrics_scrape_unauthorized)."
        labels:
          # critical, not warning (2026-10-21): a ban silently 403s EVERY
          # request from that address, and the SDN egresses through one NAT
          # address, so a single ban takes the whole private subnet out of HA
          # for as long as nobody notices (3 days, last time).
          severity: warning
        isPaused: false

      # ── Orphan-guest detector health ─────────────────────────────────────
