        annotations:
          summary: "AdGuard Home DNS is offline"
          description: "switch.adguard_home_protection has been unavailable for 2+ minutes -- AdGuard Home DNS has likely stopped, which can interrupt internet access for the whole LAN. HA's own watchdog automation attempts hassio.addon_restart on its trigger, but that trigger entity (binary_sensor.adguard_home_running) doesn't currently exist so the self-heal never actually runs -- restart manually: `ha addons restart a0d7b954_adguard` (or via the HA UI: Settings > Add-ons > AdGuard Home > Restart)."
        labels:
          severity: critical
        isPaused: false

      - uid: ecobee_pairing_down
        title: Ecobee HomeKit pairing down
        condition: C
        data:
          - refId: A
            relativeTimeRange:
              from: 300
              to: 0
            datasourceUid: victoriametrics
            model:
              datasource:
                type: prometheus
                uid: victoriametrics
              # The ecobee<->HA HomeKit pairing (Edgewater Road config
              # entry) carries the thermostat, every room sensor, and
              # every door/window contact -- including
              # binary_sensor.garage_door_contact, which the garage-lights
              # automation triggers on. When the pairing drops, ALL of
              # those go `unavailable` together (entity_available=0) and
              # door-triggered automations go silently dead for the
              # duration. The pairing flapped 101x 2026-09-24..09-30
              # (median ~55s, max ~13min per episode) during the LAN/WiFi
              # storms documented in docs/ipam.md; a recurrence would
              # otherwise be invisible until a door event was missed.
              #
              # Backstop rationale (house pattern, same as
              # smart_vent_controller_frozen): the HA-local watchdog
              # (automations/ecobee_pairing_watchdog.yaml) self-heals via
              # a config-entry reload within minutes; this rule is the
              # independent, multi-channel-routed alert (HA push +
              # Discord + email) for when the self-heal fails or HA
              # itself can't notify.
              editorMode: code
              expr: min(homeassistant_entity_available{entity=~"binary_sensor.garage_door_contact|climate.ecobee_thermostat"})
              instant: true
              intervalMs: 1000
              maxDataPoints: 43200
              range: false
              refId: A
          - refId: C
            datasourceUid: __expr__
            model:
              conditions:
                - evaluator:
                    params: [0]
                    type: eq
                  operator:
                    type: and
                  query:
                    params: [A]
                  reducer:
                    type: last
                    params: []
                  type: query
              datasource:
                name: Expression
                type: __expr__
                uid: __expr__
              expression: A
              intervalMs: 1000
              maxDataPoints: 43200
              refId: C
              type: threshold
        # noDataState=Alerting (not OK) is deliberate, like the
        # smart_vent_controller_frozen rule: if the metric stops being
        # exported entirely (HA Prometheus exporter down, entities
        # renamed, HA core down) that is ALSO a "no visibility into the
        # pairing" condition and must page rather than go silent.
        noDataState: Alerting
        execErrState: Error
        for: 10m
        annotations:
          summary: "Ecobee HomeKit pairing has been unavailable for 10+ minutes"
          description: "The Edgewater Road HomeKit pairing (thermostat + room sensors + all door/window contacts) has been unavailable for 10+ minutes -- door-triggered automations (garage/front/backyard) are degraded or dead while it is down. The HA watchdog (automations/ecobee_pairing_watchdog.yaml) should have reloaded the config entry already; if this is still firing, the self-heal didn't clear it. Check the network path to the thermostat (192.168.86.22, WiFi -- see docs/ipam.md) and the ecobee itself."
        labels:
          severity: warning
        isPaused: false

      - uid: ecobee_door_sensor_battery_low
        title: Ecobee door/window sensor battery low
        condition: C
        data:
          - refId: A
            relativeTimeRange:
              from: 300
              to: 0
            datasourceUid: victoriametrics
            model:
              datasource:
                type: prometheus
                uid: victoriametrics
              # The ecobee EBDWC01 door/window smart sensors are 915MHz
              # devices that report to the THERMOSTAT (not directly to
              # HA); HA sees them through the HomeKit pairing's battery
              # characteristic. A weakening cell degrades the 915MHz
              # hop before anything else shows symptoms -- i.e. missed
              # door-open events exactly like the occasional miss
              # investigated 2026-10-02 (light-stays-off class). The
              # contact sensors drive door-triggered automations
              # (garage lights, etc.), so catching a low cell early is
              # the software-side prevention for that failure class.
              # Same rationale as door_lock_battery_low above (early
              # threshold, per-entity instances); 30% chosen because
              # these CR2477 cells have a ~3-year nominal life and a
              # flat discharge curve -- a 30% reading means months of
              # notice, not days.
              editorMode: code
              expr: min by (entity) (homeassistant_sensor_battery_percent{entity=~"sensor.garage_door_battery_4|sensor.front_door_battery_5|sensor.backyard_door_battery"})
              instant: true
              intervalMs: 1000
              maxDataPoints: 43200
              range: false
              refId: A
          - refId: C
            datasourceUid: __expr__
            model:
              conditions:
                - evaluator:
                    params: [30]
                    type: lt
                  operator:
                    type: and
                  query:
                    params: [A]
                  reducer:
                    type: last
                    params: []
                  type: query
              datasource:
                name: Expression
                type: __expr__
                uid: __expr__
              expression: A
              intervalMs: 1000
              maxDataPoints: 43200
              refId: C
              type: threshold
        noDataState: OK
        execErrState: Error
        for: 5m
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ '{{' }} $labels.entity {{ '}}' }} battery is below 30%"
          description: "An ecobee door/window contact sensor (915MHz to the thermostat) is below 30% battery. A weakening cell misses door-open events -- the exact failure class (light stays off on an open) this replacement prevents. These are CR2477 cells with ~3-year nominal life; swap the battery at the next convenience and re-check the garage-lights automation afterward. Sensor: {{ '{{' }} $labels.entity {{ '}}' }}."
        labels:
          severity: warning
        isPaused: false

